<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="68bc2e87" lines="46-60">
<description>
Recover the bottom-to-top stacking order of overlaid rectangular color frames.

Input structure:
- The background is one color, and every foreground color is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame.
- Overlapping frame pixels show only the color of the frame that is highest at that location.

Rule:
- Reconstruct each frame as the border of the bounding box of its visible pixels.
- At every cell covered by multiple reconstructed frames, the visible frame color is above the other frames covering that cell.
- Return the unique bottom-to-top order of frame colors as a single-column grid.

Key insight:
The hidden parts of each frame do not need to be seen directly; the visible pixels reveal the full frame bbox, and overlap cells reveal the layer constraints.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="d93c6891" lines="84-101">
<description>
Move each gray bar into its adjacent orange rectangle as full stripes from the contacted edge.

Input structure
- The scene uses black outside space, yellow fill, gray one-cell-thick bars, and solid orange rectangles.
- Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.
- Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.

Rule
- Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.
- For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.
- Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.
- Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.
- Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.

Key insight
- The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="df978a02" lines="57-72">
<description>
Let the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.

Input structure
- The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects.
- The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions.
- Exactly one object has the largest area.

Rule
- Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects.
- Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background.
- Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.

Key insight
- The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="b74ca5d1" lines="157-172">
<description>
Swap each 5x5 sprite with its corner marker color and stamp same-marker composite masks into the matching corner.

Input structure:
- A 30x30 grid has one dominant background color.
- Non-background corner pixels are marker labels.
- Every other foreground color is the body of one 5x5 sprite containing exactly one pixel of a corner marker color.

Rule:
- For each sprite, recolor body pixels to the sprite marker color and recolor the marker pixel back to its body color.
- If a marker lies on the side facing its matching corner and another same-marker sprite has a body pixel at that same local cell, use that visible body color for the marker pixel.
- For each corner marker, overlay the masks of all sprites with that marker color into the corner 5x5 block and paint the visible composite with the marker color.

Key insight:
The corner pixels are labels that group 5x5 sprites by their embedded marker color; each labeled corner shows the grouped sprite silhouette.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="18286ef8" lines="17-31">
<description>
Use the pink control pixel to choose where the maroon pixel belongs inside the grey 3x3 region, and turn the control pixel maroon.

Input structure:
- The grid contains one 3x3 region made from eight grey pixels and one maroon pixel.
- There is exactly one pink control pixel outside that region.

Rule:
- Restore the old maroon cell in the 3x3 region to grey.
- Compare the pink pixel to the 3x3 region: above/same band/below selects top/middle/bottom, and left/same band/right selects left/middle/right.
- Paint the selected cell maroon and recolor the pink control pixel maroon.

Key insight:
- The pink pixel acts as a relative-position marker for a 3x3 target inside the grey region.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="cbebaa4b" lines="51-65">
<description>
Assemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.

Input structure:
- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.
- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.
- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.

Rule:
- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.
- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.
- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.

Key insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="ecb67b6d" lines="138-155">
<description>
Recolor the grey diagonal-chain regions in an orange field to teal.

Input structure
The input contains only grey pixels and orange pixels.  Grey pixels form
several 8-connected regions embedded in the orange field.

Rule
Decompose each grey 8-connected region into 4-connected lobes.  Recolor the
whole region teal only when it contains a straight diagonal run of at least
three pixels and those lobes meet only by diagonal corner contacts in a
non-branching diagonal chain or cycle; a single 4-connected region is
recolored only when it is a square diagonal staircase.

Key insight
The teal regions are not selected by size or position; they are the grey
regions whose internal connectivity depends on diagonal corner joins, while
same-edge cracks, rectangles, bars, and branching/noisy regions remain grey.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="eval" puzzle_id="9aaea919" lines="38-51">
<description>
Transfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.
- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.

Rule:
- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.
- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.
- All marker bars are removed.

Key insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="25c199f5" lines="30-44">
<description>
Pack the non-background rows from three separated panels into one panel, scanning panels right-to-left.

Input structure:
- A rectangular grid contains three equal-size panels separated by two full-height divider columns.
- The background is the dominant color; divider pixels use a color that appears only in the divider columns.

Rule:
- Read panels from right to left, and within each panel read rows from top to bottom.
- Keep each row that contains any non-background pixel, discarding all-background rows.
- Place the kept rows at the bottom of a single panel-shaped output, padding above with background rows.

Key insight:
- The task is row packing: the divider columns mark independent panels, and only the occupied rows are moved.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="84ba50d3" lines="36-54">
<description>
Drop the blue sprites downward relative to the red divider, with vertical sprites passing through to the bottom.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- One non-background color forms a single full-width horizontal divider row.
- The other non-background color forms 4-connected sprites above the divider.

Rule:
- Clear the original sprite pixels.
- A one-column sprite falls to the bottom of its column and deletes the divider
  pixel it crossed.
- Any wider sprite falls until its unique widest horizontal row is immediately
  above the divider row; if the moved sprite overlaps the divider, it covers it.

Key insight:
The red row is not a generic floor: only one-column sprites pass through to the
bottom, while wider sprites are registered by their widest horizontal segment.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="78332cb0" lines="10-23">
